  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nIt is a beautiful fall day and there are no major changes to the\n7-day forecast. A blocking high pressure will remain in place \nover the Great Lakes / eastern Canada region through early next \nweek, keeping the greatest rain chances west and south \ngenerally.\n\nThere are three main windows to watch for rain this period, with\nthe first two coming tonight into Thursday, and then Friday \ninto Saturday. In both cases, a weak shortwave will help enhance\nthe nocturnal LLJ, providing enough forcing for a broad area of\nshowers to develop. There will be limited instability with \ndewpoints stuck in the 50s, so widespread storms and heavier \nrainfall is not expected. As the rain spreads from west to east \nThursday, and southwest to northeast in the case of Saturday, \ndrier air and broad subsidence associated with the high pressure\nwill limit how far east the rain is able to travel. \n\nThe pattern will start to show signs of breaking down early next \nweek as a more pronounced shortwave nudges the upper ridge \neastward. Tied to the shortwave, a more robust low pressure \nsystem would develop at the surface, resulting in a brief warm-\nup Monday before winds shift out of the northwest and advect in \ncooler and drier air for the middle of the week.",
